WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.109 --> 00:00:05.880
Zak and Alma waited in line at the ice cream vendor's cart.

2
00:00:05.881 --> 00:00:12.110
They were taking a break from racing around the park playing Dragons Search on their cell phones.

3
00:00:12.111 --> 00:00:18.554
They'd spent two hours trying to see who could find and capture the most dragons in the game.

4
00:00:18.555 --> 00:00:24.722
"You might have the higher score now, but I'm going to win," Zak teased.

5
00:00:24.723 --> 00:00:31.654
"I doubt that." Alma smiled. "There's no way you can catch up to my score!"

6
00:00:31.655 --> 00:00:35.924
While Zak ordered, Alma noticed a man who looked familiar.

7
00:00:35.925 --> 00:00:40.069
The man was circling around, staring at his phone.

8
00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:45.942
"Is that our social studies teacher from last year, Mr. Perry?" she asked.

9
00:00:45.943 --> 00:00:53.815
Zak nodded as he paid for the ice cream.

10
00:00:53.816 --> 00:00:56.512
"Hey, Mr. Perry!" he called.

11
00:00:56.513 --> 00:01:05.729
"Phone away or it's mine for the day!" Mr. Perry used to say that when he caught students using their phones in school.

12
00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:11.867
Mr. Perry looked up and smiled. "Hi, kids. I guess you caught me!"

13
00:01:11.868 --> 00:01:16.876
"I thought you didn't like cell phones," Alma said.

14
00:01:16.877 --> 00:01:20.192
"Not in school," Mr. Perry replied. "But

15
00:01:20.193 --> 00:01:25.167
I'm thinking I might use them to teach my next unit on reading different types of maps." "You

16
00:01:25.168 --> 00:01:28.997
mean, like using a map app?" Zak asked. "Actually,

17
00:01:28.998 --> 00:01:34.346
there's a new game all the kids are playing," Mr. Perry explained. "You

18
00:01:34.347 --> 00:01:37.414
have to capture dragons and—" "You

19
00:01:37.415 --> 00:01:42.523
mean Dragons Search ?" Alma asked, showing him her phone.

20
00:01:42.524 --> 00:01:45.457
"That's it!" Mr. Perry said.

21
00:01:45.458 --> 00:01:49.533
"The game uses the Global Positioning System, or GPS.

22
00:01:49.534 --> 00:01:55.012
That's how the game knows where you're located and which dragons you can catch."

23
00:01:55.013 --> 00:01:58.647
"I know the game uses our location," Zak said.

24
00:01:58.648 --> 00:02:01.752
"But I don't understand how GPS works."

25
00:02:01.753 --> 00:02:05.483
"I'm sure you're not alone in that," Mr. Perry said.

26
00:02:05.484 --> 00:02:10.210
"GPS relies on satellites twenty-thousand kilometers above Earth.

27
00:02:10.211 --> 00:02:17.981
The satellites orbit Earth, and at any given time or place, there are around nine satellites overhead."

28
00:02:17.982 --> 00:02:23.063
"But how does my phone communicate with them?" Alma asked.

29
00:02:23.064 --> 00:02:26.446
"The satellites regularly send signals with their position.

30
00:02:26.447 --> 00:02:32.499
The GPS receivers in our devices pick up those signals," Mr. Perry said.

31
00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:37.216
"Twenty-thousand kilometers above Earth!" Alma exclaimed.

32
00:02:37.217 --> 00:02:41.961
"It must take a long time for the signals to reach Earth."

33
00:02:41.962 --> 00:02:47.787
"Nope! The signals travel very fast—at the speed of light," Mr. Perry replied.

34
00:02:47.788 --> 00:02:54.322
"Your device calculates the distance between the satellites to find the exact longitude and latitude of your location.

35
00:02:54.323 --> 00:02:59.908
The GPS receiver needs at least three satellite signals to calculate your position."

36
00:02:59.909 --> 00:03:06.617
"With so many satellites up in space, how do they know not to bump into each other?" Zak asked.

37
00:03:06.618 --> 00:03:10.021
"Good question," Mr. Perry said.

38
00:03:10.022 --> 00:03:14.610
"The satellites travel in a fixed orbit around Earth at a set rate of speed.

39
00:03:14.611 --> 00:03:19.011
The satellites and orbits are spaced to maintain a safe distance from each other.

40
00:03:19.012 --> 00:03:23.865
Each satellite takes approximately 12 hours to complete one orbit."

41
00:03:23.866 --> 00:03:28.255
"My cousins have a hobby they're always talking about," Zak said.

42
00:03:28.256 --> 00:03:34.029
"It has something to do with GPS. It starts with 'geo.'"

43
00:03:34.030 --> 00:03:37.117
"Geocaching?" Mr. Perry asked.

44
00:03:37.118 --> 00:03:43.617
"That's it!" Zak licked some ice cream as it started dripping.

45
00:03:43.618 --> 00:03:48.736
"Geocaching is like a giant treasure hunt," Mr. Perry explained.

46
00:03:48.737 --> 00:03:55.765
"It's become very popular—there are over one million geocaches hidden all over the world." "How

47
00:03:55.766 --> 00:03:59.106
do you play?" Alma asked. "Players

48
00:03:59.107 --> 00:04:01.897
choose a geocache from a list. Then,

49
00:04:01.898 --> 00:04:07.506
using the geocache's coordinates, they use GPS to find the geocache. It

50
00:04:07.507 --> 00:04:12.247
usually involves a fun hike or exploring an interesting neighborhood." "Sounds

51
00:04:12.248 --> 00:04:16.391
like Dragons Search ," Alma said.

52
00:04:16.392 --> 00:04:23.285
"Kind of. Except geocaches are real boxes with real objects inside," Mr. Perry explained.

53
00:04:23.286 --> 00:04:27.968
"If you take something out of the box, you leave something of similar value behind.

54
00:04:27.969 --> 00:04:33.065
You also sign the logbook, leaving a note for other geocachers."

55
00:04:33.066 --> 00:04:38.726
"We should ask your cousins to take us geocaching, Zak," Alma said.

56
00:04:38.727 --> 00:04:42.094
"Just remember to be respectful and safe.

57
00:04:42.095 --> 00:04:45.622
Don't roam onto private property or into dangerous terrain.

58
00:04:45.623 --> 00:04:51.725
And don't take unnecessary risks just to reach the geocache," Mr. Perry said.

59
00:04:51.726 --> 00:04:55.310
"Thanks for the lesson, Mr. Perry," Zak said.

60
00:04:55.311 --> 00:05:02.301
"When we get back to our game, I'm going to imagine signals bouncing between my phone and the GPS satellites."

61
00:05:02.302 --> 00:05:12.345
"Me too," Alma laughed. "That'll make beating Zak even more fun!"

